#!markdown

# 2.1 Using Tools

*Using Microsoft Semantic Kernel (Agent Framework) in C#*

#!markdown

By binding tools or functions to your LLM, the model gets information about those tools, and it will tell you that it wants to execute a tool. Notice the LLM is not going to execute the tool, it's the server where the process is running. That same process will also return the result of those tools back to the LLM.

This notebook demonstrates the same concepts as the LangChain version, but using Microsoft Semantic Kernel in C#.

#!csharp

#r "nuget: Microsoft.SemanticKernel, 1.32.0"
#r "nuget: DotNetEnv, 3.1.1"

using Microsoft.SemanticKernel;
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel.ChatCompletion;
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel.Connectors.OpenAI;
using System.ComponentModel;
using DotNetEnv;

#!markdown

Define the Weather Plugin

#!csharp

public class WeatherPlugin
{
    [KernelFunction, Description("Get the current weather for a specified location")]
    public string GetWeather(
        [Description("The name of the city. Must be one of: Chicago, New York, or Los Angeles")]
        string location
    )
    {
        var weatherData = new Dictionary<string, string>
        {
            { "New York", "Sunny, 25°C" },
            { "Los Angeles", "Cloudy, 22°C" },
            { "Chicago", "Rainy, 18°C" }
        };
        
        return weatherData.TryGetValue(location, out var weather) 
            ? weather 
            : "Weather data not available for this location.";
    }
}

#!markdown

Initialize the kernel and add the weather plugin

#!csharp

// Load environment variables
Env.Load();

var endpoint = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT");
var apiKey = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY");
var deploymentName = "gpt-4o-mini";

// Create kernel builder
var builder = Kernel.CreateBuilder();

// Add Azure OpenAI Chat Completion service
builder.AddAzureOpenAIChatCompletion(
    deploymentName: deploymentName,
    endpoint: endpoint,
    apiKey: apiKey
);

// Add the weather plugin
builder.Plugins.AddFromType<WeatherPlugin>();

// Build the kernel
var kernel = builder.Build();

#!markdown

Get the chat completion service

#!csharp

var chatService = kernel.GetRequiredService<IChatCompletionService>();

#!markdown

Set up the chat history

#!csharp

var chatHistory = new ChatHistory();
chatHistory.AddSystemMessage(
    "You are a helpful assistant that can provide weather information for specific cities."
);
chatHistory.AddUserMessage("What is the weather like in NYC?");

#!markdown

Call the LLM with automatic tool execution

#!csharp

// Configure execution settings to enable automatic function calling
var executionSettings = new OpenAIPromptExecutionSettings
{
    FunctionChoiceBehavior = FunctionChoiceBehavior.Auto()
};

// Get the response with automatic function calling
var response = await chatService.GetChatMessageContentAsync(
    chatHistory,
    executionSettings,
    kernel
);

chatHistory.Add(response);

#!markdown

Print the result

#!csharp

Console.WriteLine("Final response:");
Console.WriteLine(response.Content);

Console.WriteLine("\\nFull chat history:");
foreach (var message in chatHistory)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"{message.Role}: {message.Content}");
}

#!markdown

Notice how Semantic Kernel automatically handles the tool calls and returns the final response.

**NOTE:** Usage of tools means 2 LLM calls, one to know what tool is necessary and second one containing the result of the tool call
